.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH tapectl-volume-init 1  "tapectl-volume-init " 
.SH NAME
tapectl\-volume\-init \- Initialize a new volume (write ID thunk to tape)
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtapectl volume init\fR [\fB\-\-device\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-force\fR] [\fB\-n\fR|\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-\-generation\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-\-cartridge\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-quiet\fR] [\fB\-\-barcode\fR] [\fB\-y\fR|\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-\-home\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fILABEL\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Initialize a new volume (write ID thunk to tape)
.PP
After the volume line, prints one line naming the medium the cartridge was taken for: its generation, the capacity stored on the volume (decimal) and what decided each, e.g. `medium: LTO\-5, 1.50 TB (the cartridge chip\*(Aqs medium density code 0x58)`. An LTO\-5 cartridge in an LTO\-6 drive is seen to be LTO\-5 here. `\-\-json` carries the same facts as a `medium` object.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-device\fR \fI<DEVICE>\fR
Tape device (by\-id path). Defaults to the only configured drive; required when more than one is configured
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Output in JSON format
.TP
\fB\-\-force\fR
Overwrite a cartridge whose File 0 already identifies a DIFFERENT volume (e.g. a mislabeled or stale tape), or is EMPTY (a filemark at the beginning of the tape). Refused by default — loading the wrong cartridge would otherwise silently overwrite it. An EMPTY or unreadable File 0 needs no `\-\-force` when the tape is PROVEN blank (ADR\-0012 2026\-10\-15 item 57): the chip\*(Aqs lifetime totals read 0 MiB written, or this catalog\*(Aqs own `cartridge erase` verified it blank and nothing has been written since; init prints which proof it used. Never overrides a cartridge that is already SEALED (ADR\-0003): this catalog\*(Aqs own sealed volume is a copy to eject, not a blank; a sealed tape this catalog does not hold, or holds retired, is erased first with `tapectl cartridge erase` (ADR\-0012 item 216). Never degauss an LTO cartridge: that destroys its servo tracks. It also never overrides the drive/media compatibility refusal, which is a physical fact rather than a risk judgement (ADR\-0010)
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would be done without making changes. Commands that cannot preview refuse the flag rather than ignore it
.TP
\fB\-\-generation\fR \fI<GENERATION>\fR
Declare the loaded medium\*(Aqs generation (e.g. LTO\-6, LTO\-7\-M8).

Normally unnecessary and normally ignored: ADR\-0010 DETECTS the generation from the drive (MAM medium density code, else MAM format density code, else the st driver\*(Aqs density register), and a detected code is a fact about the tape that this flag cannot override — a `\-\-generation` contradicting one is an error, not a hint. It is consulted only when no source reports a recognised code.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Enable verbose output
.TP
\fB\-\-cartridge\fR \fI<CARTRIDGE>\fR
Bind this volume to an already\-registered cartridge: its barcode, or the serial its chip reports.

Normally unnecessary: `volume init` matches the loaded medium\*(Aqs chip serial to a registered cartridge, and registers the cartridge itself when nothing matches. Use this for a cartridge registered by hand before it was ever loaded, or on a drive that reports no chip serial, where it names the cartridge. A serial match wins over this flag, since the serial was read off the medium.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
No live progress on stderr. Long operations (stage create, volume write/resume/verify/read\-slices, restore) still write their session log under <home>/logs/
.TP
\fB\-\-barcode\fR \fI<BARCODE>\fR
The barcode on the cartridge\*(Aqs sticker, for a cartridge this init registers or one still wearing its chip serial as a placeholder barcode. Without it such a cartridge takes the volume\*(Aqs label as its barcode (ADR\-0012 item 219), the usual practice: the same text on the sticker and on the tape. A cartridge that already has a barcode keeps it, and a different `\-\-barcode` is refused (change a sticker with `tapectl cartridge relabel`). The chip serial stays the cartridge\*(Aqs identity either way
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Skip ADR\-0008 Tier\-2 confirmation prompts. It never reaches a Tier\-3 refusal — those are facts, not risks to accept
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<CONFIG>\fR
Path to config file.

NOTE: on its own this ALSO relocates the whole tapectl home to the config file\*(Aqs parent directory — database, keys, staging, stage reports. That is how every test harness gets an isolated home, so it still works, but it is surprising enough that it now warns. Use \-\-home when you mean "operate on a different archive", and \-\-config only to point at a config file inside that home.
.TP
\fB\-\-home\fR \fI<HOME>\fR
tapectl home directory: database, keys, catalogs, stage\-reports, logs.

Defaults to ~/.tapectl. The config file is taken from <home>/config.toml unless \-\-config overrides it. Also settable as TAPECTL_HOME.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
<\fILABEL\fR>
Volume label (e.g., L6\-0001)
